#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130052} :activity/id #uuid "b14c5a1b-dfd3-54ea-b205-2433540d7060" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130382 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}})-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." :tutorial/id #uuid "48f4e466-1052-5e98-b9a0-d345e3139cb2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3914 :tutorial/title "Defining the Differentiability of Single Variable Functions in Terms of Tangent Lines"} :step/id #uuid "41594163-d4dc-5179-be5c-4adec6fac7bc" :step/math-academy-id 23115 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130383 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0,y_0).$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nAssume that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function of two variables. Since $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0),$ then there exists a vector $\\nabla f = \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (x_0,y_0)}\\dfrac {f(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle} {||\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle||} = 0. \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFor the above limit to hold, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $(x_0,y_0),$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) - \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x,y) - f(x_0,y_0) \\approx \\langle x-x_0, y-y_0\\rangle \\cdot \\langle f_1, f_2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nExpanding the dot product, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx  f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\cdot f_1+ (y-y_0)\\cdot f_2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to show that $f_1$ and $f_2$ are the partial derivatives.\n\n- Let $y=y_0$ in the first equation $(\\ast)$ above. This gives\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x,y_0) - f(x_0,y_0) - (x-x_0) f_1} {x-x_0} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is the definition of the partial derivative with respect to $x.$ So, we have $f_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- Similarly, letting $x = x_0$ gives $f_2 = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ can be linearized as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\\approx f(x_0,y_0) + (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nBy setting $z=f(x,y)$ and $z_0 = f(x_0,y_0),$ we get the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at $(x_0,y_0)$ *if the function admits a unique tangent plane* at $(x_0, y_0).$\n\nFinally, note that we have also shown that \n\n$$\n\\nabla f = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}, \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\nabla f$ is known as the **gradient vector**." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e6cb319-460f-5a22-888e-e3f886ea9b0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3915 :tutorial/title "Defining the Differentiability of Multivariable Functions in Terms of Tangent Planes"} :step/id #uuid "c0a78a51-4800-5622-8fa8-92069e9e72f6" :step/math-academy-id 23116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128996 :question/id #uuid "a6d910ae-c6db-51d2-93ba-f6c823c0a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4de868c0-80b7-5cbb-9dc2-ba04493471fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504673}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Plane"} :step/id #uuid "6b19e61c-814d-510b-93fb-964a9df12d5a" :step/math-academy-id 23118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128997 :question/id #uuid "4eb4806c-3d5b-5ed2-87a1-d1ed50c267e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6182"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538936}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties"} :step/id #uuid "a126e19a-f8b5-5040-9719-0bc0a4c611e3" :step/math-academy-id 23123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130044}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130384 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z = f(x,y)$ at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be given as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nSlightly rewriting the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (x-x_0) + {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)} \\cdot (y-y_0) + ({\\color{blue}-1}) \\cdot (z - z_0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich immediately gives us a normal vector to the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)}, \\:\\: {\\color{blue}-1} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the tangent plane to the surface is called a **normal vector to the surface**." :tutorial/id #uuid "c5ebd9d6-c219-583a-8bca-87268b8aa8bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3919 :tutorial/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Surface"} :step/id #uuid "828fdb16-4d33-565c-9b6f-4db12aed5567" :step/math-academy-id 23133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130385 :tutorial/content "Until now, we considered only functions of the form \n\n![](images/48/4838bea41cd622ef69be5caaafac89df818d35cf108730686d68d7b7a12265ac.svg)\n\nof two independent variables. But what about the case of functions \n\n![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg)\n\nwith three variables? Can we define a tangent plane to such a function?\n\nThe answer is yes! Analogously to the case with two variables, a function of three variables ![](images/72/72e701a6fcd4514adb9731764fc74a1cb3f0b73f548680db162cd2c8a025e0d6.svg) is differentiable at ![](images/f4/f46650c560f32308db30c404d86da2d5921654805cca770cd2bc3627e55dbf86.svg) if it admits a **unique tangent hyperplane** at ![](images/3f/3fe19998bf33d149c699a01ca363c2691041b3ecfd52c47a167053b4097dbb57.svg) where ![](images/7e/7e2db5c48c69709e3c1d04d4834af7cc0b2ff0df186cf1713e207c164a8e41e5.svg) The equation of the **tangent hyperplane** is given by\n\n![](images/f4/f433839875810dc7417400a22bad7b8a63c0eb1033954a8e91e7049f3ada361a.svg)\n\nNotice that we use the term *hyperplane*. The graphs of functions of three variables live in four-dimensional space. The equation above describes an object (a so-called hyperplane) in four-dimensional space that can be considered an analog to a *plane* in three-dimensional space." :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f087f4-e5d4-59f8-90f7-a5a9484a0c6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3920 :tutorial/title "The Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"} :step/id #uuid "773e70e4-737a-5fa4-92ed-0e1d04815ea8" :step/math-academy-id 23134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128999 :question/id #uuid "ed1a3960-c2c4-57e7-8fd1-daf5d98bf052" :question/math-academy-id "e-6185"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc56f7a5-65a7-53a9-9eb6-ae7b91a1bdd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504633}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Hyperplane"} :step/id #uuid "ca4b822d-4434-5d75-8400-5106f71e78a9" :step/math-academy-id 23135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130386 :tutorial/content "Similar to tangent lines for single variable functions, we can think of the **tangent plane** to a surface at a point as the plane that \"touches\" the surface at that point.\n\nThe equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the surface \n\n$$\nz = 4-x^2-y^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface at the point $(0,\\sqrt{2},2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03df90a94615238e2126338f64d1f51fcefc379068a8cdc366b33b998cd69f45.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y) = (0,\\sqrt{2})\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) & =-2(\\sqrt{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-2 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,\\sqrt{2})+(y-\\sqrt{2})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =0(x-0)-2\\sqrt{2}(y-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =-2\\sqrt{2}y+4 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}y+z-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane is $2\\sqrt{2}y + z - 6 = 0.$\n\n![](images/d5/d51bdbe8820472334f7e1d942c027b6ad8164eaece58220f3f2e5cdb51544f05.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bced0695-3ebd-5b62-b7ff-9c8f2500fc2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3921 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0355e3e0-f89b-5dca-97bc-21791fa4aed2" :step/math-academy-id 23153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130396 :tutorial/content "A surface $z = f(x,y)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent plane at $P.$ Intuitively, this means that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the surface $z=f(x,y)$ can be approximated as a plane.\n\nNote that this is analogous to the case of functions of one variable. A function $y = f(x)$ is differentiable at a point $P$ if and only if it admits a *unique* tangent *line* at $P.$ This is the same as saying that, in the neighborhood of $P,$ the curve $y=f(x)$ can be approximated as a straight line.  \n\nWe'll explore these ideas in more depth at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at computing tangent planes." :tutorial/id #uuid "015c4e8e-c1ba-5672-8228-ff0869681d6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4231 :tutorial/title "A Note on Differentiability"} :step/id #uuid "0296b5b5-c2ce-56c6-a8e4-ad0af71e0dde" :step/math-academy-id 25668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130046}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129328}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407515} :activity/id #uuid "710af205-0718-5ccf-b346-4f23b266524d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52002c6b-9859-5b7b-a1de-586418b14736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,3,28)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339881} {:answer/id #uuid "551d2228-56ee-5488-91bb-0169794e5cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339910} {:answer/id #uuid "796a4945-abaa-5bd2-997d-087f2262b1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340210} {:answer/id #uuid "8a645bd4-5f4d-5164-aa70-44b211e15ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(-1,3,28)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340364} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b33a5b-7afb-5095-9268-a98f89267aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-3,0)$ and $(3,1,20)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339910} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58edcff-ee66-5f0c-86cd-1ec279fff31c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c589653c-432a-5bb8-acb2-94698429fbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73857" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2 &=0 \\\\ 2y+6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "0eadd7d5-26a1-573f-b533-83cfbfb498b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464dff10-0c59-5dad-80a1-2ee16d60dbfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,-\\pi ,2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321703} {:answer/id #uuid "48223b68-6d40-5d37-8bb5-b27364a9cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321715} {:answer/id #uuid "a226c677-0c51-577f-b0f5-1b3064cc6815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322427} {:answer/id #uuid "adf98dbd-c12a-5550-9749-ddcc015f00fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2\\pi ,\\pi ,12\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322512} {:answer/id #uuid "d80a3e3a-b37d-53fa-b4ba-7e5d51839fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,6,8\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322427} :answer-field/id #uuid "5baf0726-2cff-5a6b-904f-2992e085ab37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c9333a9-b5d6-5ec8-b1f3-0c62768fb8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37986" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy &=8 \\\\ -xc &=2\\pi \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$"} :step/id #uuid "2b51d9b4-6f4d-519a-946f-18af30633fc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4831fde7-328b-5f95-8b4f-bd555404f79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263624} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9d3b86-7aec-5970-92c9-47d3d544786f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263789} {:answer/id #uuid "94d3b3cb-73a3-5f94-abc9-28adc6612ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264274} {:answer/id #uuid "9d20a40f-b237-5738-bfa6-76f889ea8789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264345} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e3749-cf7d-5ffc-ac93-9256ee36c08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264345} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b8a09a-7de4-5c87-b2a5-460a354244c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "12830aeb-cb43-530e-902c-e52ba9d7524f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129557" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6cx &=4 \\\\ 2cy &=-2 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$"} :step/id #uuid "32cd70ef-6665-5a4e-a0ab-b70e5098e66f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62fcc3f3-ddff-52b4-b6ee-b406a0ffec98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y-z+w+\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340008} {:answer/id #uuid "93a5ca28-3798-5c54-9d92-aa2d41eb6d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi x-\\pi y+z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340453} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8794dd-7491-5377-b367-530775903444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z-w+3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340570} {:answer/id #uuid "f6251148-2e5e-526a-995c-c61130894b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y-z+w-\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341302} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7d3452-6743-53e2-bd9c-8cfa857fa7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi =0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341343} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31f7eac-9e1f-5aaa-97e4-e132312fc936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de6bd964-56b9-511c-9d67-ed8d06732a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-73851" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ at the point $(1,1,\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) & =(1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\pi )=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,\\pi )+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,\\pi )+(z-\\pi )\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi (x-1)-\\pi (y-1)-(z-\\pi ) \\\\ w & =-\\pi x+\\pi -\\pi y+\\pi -z+\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x+\\pi y+z+w-3\\pi  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "536015a5-6f9f-597a-ae46-2c87559ba2b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407514}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf0dc93-d9d4-5f21-8930-dd5004fc42a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407507} {:answer/id #uuid "5110fa49-48ce-56a6-b93d-b9d08b07b745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y+2z-w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407508} {:answer/id #uuid "728a521d-8a62-5203-8384-b89408abe64b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y-2z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407509} {:answer/id #uuid "72b11d02-8ab8-5c20-a244-f44e993b6792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-2z-w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407510} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e8ca25-16b2-54a8-b96c-ad5665775b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y+2z+w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407507} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cea473-3250-5a97-b3ad-a51baa102dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ca7d6fc-55d0-503c-8138-64f03d668ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73850" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ at the point $(1,-1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}))=-\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}-(1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y+1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)-2(y+1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2-2y-2-2z+2 \\\\ -2x+2y+2z+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y-2z-w-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93f5b13c-94e0-5cb3-a51d-2c9c6f5ed98b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407517}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407506}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435558} :activity/id #uuid "b048708b-7094-537d-b288-e2f229bf17e6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e1eeb8-4189-5718-8f50-bdf2f86033b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+15y+16z-w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435526} {:answer/id #uuid "7147124b-3c14-59fb-b95d-12d323b0e513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+15y+16z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435531} {:answer/id #uuid "df2eecc4-dc64-5c9b-8ab8-81624c44bb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-15y+16z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435541} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dc19b2-fcd8-593e-b5c7-c02d25ef9070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x+15y-16z+w-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435542} {:answer/id #uuid "f64cc117-a5b0-5386-a6c5-f6d385b6497f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-15y-16z+w+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6aa0ef9-33dc-511a-a8b4-7535395d3236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923d3b9b-11dc-5749-a7e2-257ace8090ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73849" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z$ at the point $(2,-2,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=2{y}^{2}-6xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=4xy+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}z)=2yz-3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,-2,1) & =2(-2)^{2}-6(2)(1)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,-2,1) & =4(2)(-2)+(1)^{2}=-15 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,-2,1) & =2(-2)(1)-3(2)^{2}=-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,-2,1)+(y+2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,-2,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,-2,1) \\\\ w-2 & =-4(x-2)-15(y+2)-16(z-1) \\\\ w-2 & =-4x+8-15y-30-16z+16 \\\\ 4x+15y+16z+w+4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0af89c86-dd82-51d3-8a60-9428fdaf17c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6521128c-8b33-54da-8c83-d27bef687244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435529} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4d22-52c4-582f-b256-0ddd6fa135ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435530} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf53e7-7029-5a30-8dd0-ad54d0eb0064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435533} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd79e5d-b439-5f83-a72f-40d75fd4cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-1,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435537} {:answer/id #uuid "d89ebc6c-8c25-5ee8-a102-c0421505fca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1008d1e-ad25-59f1-8249-03f1bd3c30fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0b70f07-48f3-5e91-bb4d-83ea4566f9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129560" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-10y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,-5,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,-5,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4cx &=-2 \\\\ -10cy &=-5 \\\\ -c &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-4(-1)x=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-10(-1)y=-5\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4-2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4}).$"} :step/id #uuid "52566cd2-c610-5b7e-a93b-8dfb407f2200"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18541d05-49d2-5f19-b579-6b534b3bcfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y+z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435522} {:answer/id #uuid "1e19600e-76b4-5880-a603-02783cbb324e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-2z-w+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} {:answer/id #uuid "36b42409-fb4f-52cb-a983-864ab8cc5f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+z-w-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435525} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f12775-7dc0-5662-9058-bbd97ad38d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2y-z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435536} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2e0787-ad3a-5c7f-add5-4e016dc6c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-3z+w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b834ec-93e3-593b-87ef-065fbe59d9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b798538-8660-5c1f-9d93-5bdad8bdb40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129477" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x+y-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y-{z}^{2})=-2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) & =-2(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) \\\\ w & =(x-1)+y-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =x+y-2z+1 \\\\ x+y-2z-w+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8189ee1b-e555-5395-af62-45720087b2f5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032b951d-051b-5c7e-a89c-9ad494e6d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,-2,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435520} {:answer/id #uuid "0692ed8e-a531-5991-a793-f96a4aa13ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435521} {:answer/id #uuid "81bbb1e3-0f5e-5556-b6d5-5d35199efe2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435532} {:answer/id #uuid "af437ebd-7c23-5075-a34e-67693069d1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435535} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd4290-3c2e-59d1-86ec-25b254194b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435520} :answer-field/id #uuid "93540a4d-f358-5551-98bf-028208b64b0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1863d48a-c899-5d14-89ee-82ba61f0f79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129559" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨6,-8,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-7)=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨6,-8,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,-1⟩=⟨6,-8,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2cx &=6 \\\\ 4cy &=-8 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(1)x=6\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n4(1)y=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3$ and $y=-2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={3}^{2}+2(-2)^{2}-7 \\\\  & =9+8-7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(3,-2,10).$"} :step/id #uuid "9be88cd8-1668-517f-a1dd-283be301d1d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016f6151-b920-51fb-9d38-ba687d166468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435519} {:answer/id #uuid "2809590b-c7a6-55d5-a41a-10d24affb73e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435524} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7f9f81-4423-520f-8c2d-93184d954cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435527} {:answer/id #uuid "60e34da5-4c5d-590d-b3f8-3cc87ff70f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435528} {:answer/id #uuid "9d343591-1c30-58e2-ad9e-f585b09e607e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435519} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ae7c04-0a9c-587c-af8d-7844f40ca12a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "986c989d-58b2-593e-8458-dc3fb233ef93" :question/math-academy-id "q-73840" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}$ at the point $(0,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(0,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0) & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0) & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+0)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-0)+1(y-0) \\\\ z-1 & =x+y \\\\ -x-y+z-1 & =0 \\\\ x+y-z+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a871d925-4e2d-5145-8579-f86cf5c4e2f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435554}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435518}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510508} :activity/id #uuid "238ccd9a-3841-5932-a57b-eab79e3279ba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18541d05-49d2-5f19-b579-6b534b3bcfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y+z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435522} {:answer/id #uuid "1e19600e-76b4-5880-a603-02783cbb324e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-2z-w+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} {:answer/id #uuid "36b42409-fb4f-52cb-a983-864ab8cc5f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+z-w-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435525} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f12775-7dc0-5662-9058-bbd97ad38d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2y-z+w-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435536} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2e0787-ad3a-5c7f-add5-4e016dc6c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-3z+w+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435523} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b834ec-93e3-593b-87ef-065fbe59d9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b798538-8660-5c1f-9d93-5bdad8bdb40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129477" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=x+y-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y-{z}^{2})=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y-{z}^{2})=-2z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,0,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) & =-2(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0,1)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,0,1) \\\\ w & =(x-1)+y-2(z-1) \\\\ w & =x+y-2z+1 \\\\ x+y-2z-w+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "124b0422-9a11-52fc-b23f-2b89b132f87d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a92e8db8-6269-551b-aa1e-62c29b8cd052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,e,-1)$ and $(1,e,e-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402583} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcaac91-a08d-5d50-b23f-ff424e9006d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,1,6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402584} {:answer/id #uuid "decc010d-972c-5e29-a513-edb14a0563a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,3)$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402585} {:answer/id #uuid "d5917f2b-570f-54c3-8ea6-d8b9cbf5fbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402586} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4df4a8-f560-557c-ba34-1d929f1ab145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402584} :answer-field/id #uuid "34dfa067-c400-5022-aa9f-0f008be7be87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92e52e05-2a21-5219-b9c8-0556f35cdaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37982" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨3,5,-1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy &=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y} &=5 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$"} :step/id #uuid "593fae8f-a68f-597a-b179-b3261c8ea79e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14251a03-b1b0-5ac8-a6b0-8e82d37f60ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y+z-2w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339306} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ffcc9-fb3a-5477-b6a4-f740f112072a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-z-2w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339523} {:answer/id #uuid "3828bc36-1ffa-5912-9f56-626d879cb2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-z+2w-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339632} {:answer/id #uuid "76968a25-d5fd-5bfe-851e-0bef52e8fad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y+2z-2w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340185} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce18f8-c0ef-5d56-92ea-1b3a22b9548b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y-z+2w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339306} :answer-field/id #uuid "886a29d7-3a2d-503e-b169-7db643f5c87d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f93b9177-85da-5370-94ac-b27b761710c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73854" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{xyz}$ at the point $(2,2,4,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{yz}}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{xz}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,2,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 4}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 4}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-4 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,4)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,4) \\\\ w-4 & =(x-2)+(y-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(z-4) \\\\ w-4 & =x+y+\\frac{z}{2}-6 \\\\ -x-y-\\frac{z}{2}+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y+z-2w-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "78cf1ff6-c2f5-53cb-b4d5-d383c100c0ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7c281c-3ed2-505e-bb39-624565383d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1,1)$ and $(2,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339538} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0e6d85-901c-5c92-bee2-2a7c391f4ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339965} {:answer/id #uuid "92fe27bc-7297-5bff-aa40-85efb366b3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340449} {:answer/id #uuid "b73decc5-bf30-5593-9a84-120c9cd31d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1,1)$ and $(1,-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340784} {:answer/id #uuid "e5166059-3d34-56f7-b88e-8c219479020b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339965} :answer-field/id #uuid "169b7bd1-0afe-5339-a2e8-b0915ac47bda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9d930cb5-4a33-5f68-a149-002500eed8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73868" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨0,2,-2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨0,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨0,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{ln}⁡y &=0 \\\\ \\frac{cx}{y}+2cy &=2 \\\\ -c &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=2$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{1}+2(2)(1)=2\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-1,1,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7e867a4e-166b-5049-88c2-c6b51f97a528" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d287000-354e-5326-9c17-1a60356ce651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-z+w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339249} {:answer/id #uuid "48044e1d-d3aa-5ed8-a17b-609556187f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+z+w-12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339769} {:answer/id #uuid "89fd8971-4cae-5bee-8c4d-2b478928fdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-z-w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340360} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbc10d4-f06e-5a6d-b622-4fd3f56ab4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340391} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6a45f3-ce32-520b-8023-4080f38367c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340391} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f40803b-df03-53ae-bb29-eea5e2d21ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a68ff90-b820-5db0-8c2c-29ec6a4e0d46" :question/math-academy-id "q-73852" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx}$ at the point $(2,2,2,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+\\sqrt{zx})=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{z}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,2,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,2) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-6 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,2)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,2)+(z-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,2) \\\\ w-6 & =(x-2)+(y-2)+(z-2) \\\\ w-6 & =x+y+z-6 \\\\ -x-y-z+w & =0 \\\\ x+y+z-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ee20eb7f-6214-5a77-9f6d-2cc21ca2637f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510518}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509679}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539115} :activity/id #uuid "54d27f54-889d-5c5d-83de-87ccc0d716e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293 :topic/id #uuid "3d717848-5743-5882-aa66-adea2ee38d55" :topic/math-academy-id 1980} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6521128c-8b33-54da-8c83-d27bef687244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435529} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4d22-52c4-582f-b256-0ddd6fa135ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435530} {:answer/id #uuid "84cf53e7-7029-5a30-8dd0-ad54d0eb0064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435533} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd79e5d-b439-5f83-a72f-40d75fd4cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-1,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435537} {:answer/id #uuid "d89ebc6c-8c25-5ee8-a102-c0421505fca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435540} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1008d1e-ad25-59f1-8249-03f1bd3c30fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e0b70f07-48f3-5e91-bb4d-83ea4566f9c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129560" :question/problem "At which point on the surface $z=4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2{x}^{2}-5{y}^{2})=-10y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,-5,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨-4x,\\,-10y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,-5,1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4cx &=-2 \\\\ -10cy &=-5 \\\\ -c &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-4(-1)x=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-10(-1)y=-5\\,⟹\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =4-2{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4}).$"} :step/id #uuid "2b5d0276-eb2f-5df6-bfcf-46f02d9b5cdd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11da6c8b-9969-5900-9b7a-9809072650d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x+2y-3z-12w+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} {:answer/id #uuid "65aca46c-892b-57cc-bbda-306a11d68a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+z-4w+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263880} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fbfa20-0a93-5e20-ad08-85bb7ababd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-z-8w+18=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264558} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3eeee-85c8-5935-bbbf-97214b8aff26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-y-2z-4w+22=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264650} {:answer/id #uuid "df92e64a-c76e-5206-b481-adf89451351c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x-y-2z+6w-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263165} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0ebee6-3d61-5917-b532-d12fc16fdb57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba77fa6c-6a35-5c61-ab53-8720f1df1a35" :question/math-academy-id "q-129476" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z}$ at the point $(1,9,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{z})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,9,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{9}}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,9,4)+(y-9)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,9,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,9,4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(y-9)-\\frac{1}{4}(z-4) \\\\ w-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1 & =0 \\\\ 12(\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{6}y-\\frac{1}{4}z-w+1) & =12\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6x+2y-3z-12w+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a247dce-1016-5a74-8aa3-baeeb529100f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c645d1ad-7209-5343-8f48-3586d833b4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403726} {:answer/id #uuid "d2196f73-bb73-51d9-8fdd-442f665c7869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0,-1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403727} {:answer/id #uuid "af9a2b62-523f-5ba8-b214-6d70686e54b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,0,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403728} {:answer/id #uuid "892681cb-994a-563c-a47e-7b8ed91ea9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403729} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfb3a1a-3a3a-528f-9c59-2f8a8f352f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ and $(2,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403729} :answer-field/id #uuid "72aa1076-cce3-53b6-97d2-101a723b64a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4dda47ef-07a9-5b66-b0c0-061f88baf1dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73869" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨-2,4,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y} &=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy &=4 \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "4ceff0c8-08e1-5f7b-8d92-4c7255be7291" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16da06c8-5818-5d83-b103-468e96d457f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538281} {:answer/id #uuid "28af7c9a-9d9e-5376-9c5b-6d7fa5e689d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} {:answer/id #uuid "84475504-c2ca-5622-a840-6d61ac41fdbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538534} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9f2d3f-0ff9-53c5-b3cd-a63b7c043f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538607} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7c6f53-ab06-5a01-be5d-aa83ddab04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2,9)$ and $(1,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538317} :answer-field/id #uuid "560ffd1b-5a84-5953-b5a7-cc2f4832a155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "278532bd-d5b2-5391-ac62-5dd37f8bae10" :question/math-academy-id "q-129553" :question/problem "At which points on the surface $z=8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$"} :step/id #uuid "9423629b-5335-5a54-8c35-42763251427c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5818aebe-14d2-5cfc-9808-7df8a5a99d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y+4z-w-8=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385587} {:answer/id #uuid "ba61275e-1282-5b92-8fbf-13fc0a1de178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y+4z-w-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385588} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a3e7eb-d02d-5b58-99ab-47beb608be27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-4z+w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385589} {:answer/id #uuid "3817da57-edc7-5c07-a945-5c744c30b6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+2y-4z-w=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385590} {:answer/id #uuid "b02a4bb9-e8c1-55dd-b5e1-8d443c34fb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-2y-4z+w+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385590} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c44c80b-fac5-529e-a9a1-b7e072fc7f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ac97e9a-8ad0-507c-9c2d-206be970e2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73845" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})=-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & =2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =-4\\cdot 1=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w & =2(x-1)+2(y-1)-4(z-1) \\\\ w & =2x-2+2y-2-4z+4 \\\\ -2x-2y+4z+w & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y-4z-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1913391-3335-5bf3-ba14-e044d24b9294" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539057}}] :activity/title "Tangent Planes to Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538221}]}
